<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout>
    <title>Section 1.7: Inverse Functions</title>

    <p>
        <alert>MTH 124</alert>
    </p>

    <definition workspace="0.0in">
        <title>Definition 1.7.1</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                Let <m>f:A\rightarrow B</m> be a function.
                If there exists a function <m>g:B\rightarrow A</m> such that
                <md>
                    g(f(a)) = a, \ \ \ \text{ and } \ \ \ f(g(b))=b
                </md>
                for each <m>a</m> in <m>A</m> and each <m>b</m> in <m>B</m>,
                then we say that <m>f</m> has an <term>inverse function</term>
                and that the function <m>g</m> is
                <term>the inverse of <m>f</m>.</term>
            </p>

            <p>
                (In other words, they swap inputs and outputs and undo each
                other!)
            </p>
        </statement>
    </definition>

    <p workspace="0.0in">
        <term>Big picture:</term> if <m>y=f(x)</m> is a function with an
        inverse, <m>f(x)</m> inputs <m>x</m> and outputs <m>y</m>.
        So the inverse of <m>f</m> would input <fillin/> and output <fillin/>.
    </p>
 

    <p workspace="0.0in">
        Notation: We write the inverse of <m>f</m> as <m>f^{-1}</m>, and we read
        it as "<m>f</m> inverse".
        Essentially, the inverse of a function <q>undoes</q> the original
        function, so you get back to where you started.
    </p>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.7.2</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Recall Dolbear’s function <m>{F = D(N) = 40 + \frac14N}</m> that
                converts the number, <m>N</m>, of snowy tree cricket chirps per
                minute to a corresponding Fahrenheit temperature.
                We have earlier established that the domain of <m>D</m> is
                <m>[40,180]</m> and the range of <m>D</m> is <m>[50,85]</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.8in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Solve the equation <m>{F = 40+\frac14N}</m> for <m>N</m> in
                    terms of <m>F</m>.
                    (That is we should get <m>N=</m> stuff in terms of
                    <m>F</m>.) Call the resulting function <m>N=E(F)</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    Rearrange and solve for <m>N</m>, so 
                    <md>
                        <mrow>F \amp = 40+\frac14N </mrow>
                        <mrow>F-40 \amp = \frac14N</mrow>
                        <mrow>4(F-40) \amp = N </mrow>
                    </md>
                    so in other words we have a new function <m>N=E(F)=4(F-40)</m> that
                    inputs <m>F</m> and outputs <m>N</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.8in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Explain in words the process or effect of the function
                    <m>N=E(F)</m>.
                    What does it take as input?
                    What does it generate as output?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    This new function inputs the temperature in degrees F and 
                    outputs the number of chirps per minute.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.8in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use the function <m>E</m> that you found in (a.) to compute
                    <m>E(D(N))</m>.
                    Simplify your result as much as possible.
                    Do likewise for <m>D(E(F))</m>.
                    What do you notice about these two composite functions?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                   Replace <m>F</m> with <m>D(N)=40+\frac14N</m> in the function <m>E(F)</m>, so
                    <md>
                        <mrow> E(D(N)) \amp= E(40+\frac14N)</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp  = 4\left((40+\frac14N) - 40\right)</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 4(\frac14N) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = N </mrow>
                    </md>
                    and similarly for <m>D(E(F))</m> replace the <m>N</m>
                    in <m>D(N)</m> with <m>E(F)=4(F-40)</m>. So
                    <md>
                        <mrow>D(E(F)) \amp = D(4(F-40)) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 40+\frac14\left(4(F-40)\right) </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = 40+F-40 </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp =F.</mrow>
                    </md>
                    The <m>F/N</m> you plug in is the same thing you get out!
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.8in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the equations <m>F = 40+\frac14N</m> and
                    <m>N=4(F-40)</m>.
                    Do these equations express different relationships between
                    <m>F</m> and <m>N</m>, or do they express the same
                    relationship in two different ways?
                    Explain.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    It's the same relationship from a different perspective. 
                    For one you know <m>N</m> and find the temp <m>F</m>, 
                    for the other you know the temp <m>F</m> and find the number 
                    of chirps per minute <m>N</m>.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

      <p workspace="0in">
      Note:  You've likely been taught to switch the 
      variables.  The reason is usually so you can
      plot them.  But not switching them can often help
      us understand the meaning of the inverse function.
    </p>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Two perspectives from a function and its inverse function</title>

        <p>
            If <m>y=f(t)</m> has an inverse function, then the equations
            <md>
                y=f(t) \text{  and  } t=f^{-1}(y)
            </md>
            say the exact same thing but from two different perspectives.
        </p>
    </assemblage>
  

    <assemblage >
        <title>Horizontal Line Test</title>

        <p>
            A function whose graph lies in the <m>x</m>- <m>y</m> plane has a
            corresponding inverse function if and only if every horizontal line
            intersects the graph at most once.
            When the graph passes this test, the horizontal coordinate of each
            point on the graph can be viewed as a function of the vertical
            coordinate of the point.
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    <p workspace="0.0cm">
        Think about it in terms of inputs and outputs.
        An inverse function flips the input and the output, so what was formerly
        on the <m>y-</m> axis is now the input, and what was formerly on the
        <m>x-</m> axis is now the output.
    </p>

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.7.3. Determining if a function has an inverse</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Each relationship below describes a function.
                Determine whether the function has an inverse function.
                If it does give two examples of values of the inverse function
                by writing statements such as “ <m>{s^{-1}(3) = 1}</m> ”.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The function <m>f:S\rightarrow S</m> given by the table
                    below, where <m>S={0,1,2,3,4}</m>.
                </p>

                <tabular>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>f(x)</m></cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    No inverse function.  The inverse would need to input
                    <m>f(x)</m> and output <m>x</m>, but if we tried to
                    start with the 2 for <m>f(x)</m> it would sometimes 
                    give us <m>x=1</m> and sometimes <m>x=4</m>. In other words, 
                    <m>{f^{-1}(2) = 1 \text{ or } 4}</m>, we can't do that.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The function <m>g:S\rightarrow S</m> given by the table
                    below, where <m>S={0,1,2,3,4}</m>.
                </p>

                <tabular>
                    <row bottom="minor">
                        <cell><m>x</m></cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                    </row>

                    <row>
                        <cell><m>g(x)</m></cell>
                        <cell>4</cell>
                        <cell>0</cell>
                        <cell>3</cell>
                        <cell>1</cell>
                        <cell>2</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    Yes! We have an inverse function!  Each <m>g(x)</m>
                    has a unique corresponding <m>x</m>!  Ex: <m>g^{-1}(4)=0</m>, 
                    <m>g^{-1}(0)=1</m>, <m>g^{-1}(3)=2</m>, ...
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The function <m>p</m> given by <m>p(t)=7-\frac35t</m>.
                    Assume that the domain and codomain of <m>p</m> are both
                    “all real numbers”.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    Yes inverse function!  There are multiple ways to solve, here's two.
                
                </p>
                <p>
                    1. You could plot it and check the horizontal line test.  It passes, we 
                    have an inverse!
                </p>
                <p>
                    2. You could try to solve for <m>t</m> and see if we can do that. (Sometimes this
                    is not obvious, but here it's ok.) Rearranging we have 
                    <m>{t=-\frac53(p(t)-7)}</m>, which is totally fine!  We can find 
                    one equation for <m>t</m>, which represents the inverse function.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.7in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The function <m>q</m> given by <m>q(t)=7-\frac35t^4</m>.
                    Assume that the domain and codomain of <m>q</m> are both
                    “all real numbers”.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    No inverse.  Again, to show two ways:
                </p>
                <p>
                    1. Plot it.  It fails the horizontal line test.  Sad.  No inverse.
                </p>
                <p>
                    2. Try to solve for <m>t</m>. We would get 
                    <m>{t^4 = -\frac53(p(t)-7)}</m>, so either we have 
                    <m>{t = + (-\frac53(p(t)-7))}</m> or 
                    <m>{t = - (-\frac53(p(t)-7))}</m>, but there is more than
                    one solution for <m>t</m>!  No inverse.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The functions <m>r</m> and <m>s</m> given by the graphs
                    below.
                    Assume that the graphs show all of the important behavior of
                    the functions and that the apparent trends continue beyond
                    what is pictured.
                </p>

                <sidebyside widths="25% 25%" margins="0% 0%">
                    <image >
                        <shortdescription>
                            A function to determine if it has an inverse.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-7-3-1">
                            <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="10">
                                <definition>
                                    r(x)=(2/-(1/(1+e^(-1))-1/2))*(1/(1+e^(-x))-1/2)
                                </definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="[-5,-5,5,5]">
                                    <grid-axes/>
                                    <graph function="r"/>
                                    <label anchor="(-2,4.5)">
                                        <m>
                                            y = r(t)
                                        </m>
                                    </label>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>

                    <image>
                        <shortdescription>
                            A function to determine if it has an inverse.
                        </shortdescription>
                        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-7-3-2">
                            <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="10">
                                <definition>
                                    f(x)=-2*(x+3)+1
                                </definition>

                                <definition>
                                    g(x)=2/3*(x+3)-2
                                </definition>

                                <definition>
                                    h(x)=-1*(x-3)-3
                                </definition>
                                <coordinates bbox="[-5,-5,5,5]">
                                    <grid-axes/>
                                    <graph function="f" domain="(-5,-3)"/>
                                    <graph function="g" domain="(-3,3)"/>
                                    <graph function="h" domain="(3,5)"/>
                                    <point p="(-3,1)" fill="white"/>
                                    <point p="(3,2)" fill="white"/>
                                    <point p="(-3,-2)" fill="blue"/>
                                    <point p="(3,-3)" fill="blue"/>
                                    <label anchor="(-2,4.5)">
                                        <m>
                                            y = s(t)
                                        </m>
                                    </label>
                                </coordinates>
                            </diagram>
                        </prefigure>
                    </image>
                </sidebyside>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    The left has an inverse function because it passes the 
                    horizontal line test!  Some examples: <m>r^{-1}(2)=-1</m>, 
                    <m>r^{-1}(0)=0</m>, <m>r^{-1}(-2)=1</m>, ...
                </p>
                <p>
                    The right has no inverse function since it fails the 
                    horizontal line test.  Ye can draw a line 
                    around <m>y=1.5</m> for example and see that the 
                    y-coordinate corresponds to 2 x-coordinates here.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>

    <p>
        Read carefully and check if each property makes sense:
    </p>

    <assemblage>
        <title>Properties of an inverse function</title>

        <p>
            Let <m>f</m> be a function with an inverse function, <m>f^{-1}</m>.
            Then:
        </p>

        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        if <m>f</m> has domain <m>A</m> and range <m>B</m>, then
                        <m>f^{-1}</m> has domain <m>B</m> and range <m>A</m>
                        (domain and range swap!)
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        The functions <m>f</m> and <m>f^{-1}</m>
                        <em>reverse one anothers’ processes</em>.
                        Symbolically, <m>f^{-1}(f(a))=a</m> for every input
                        <m>a</m> in the domain of <m>f</m>, and similarly,
                        <m>f(f^{-1}(b))=b</m> for every input <m>b</m> in the
                        domain of <m>f^{-1}</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        Both <m>f</m> and <m>f^{-1}</m> express the exact same
                        relationship from a different perspective.
                    </p>
                </li>

                <li>
                    <p>
                        If <m>y=f(x)</m>, then the points <m>(x,y)</m> lies on
                        the graph of <m>f</m>.
                        Then <m>{x=f^{-1}(y)}</m>, and the point <m>(y,x)</m> lies
                        on the graph of <m>f^{-1}</m>. (If <m>(1,0)</m> is a point
                        for <m>f</m>, then <m>(0,1)</m> is a point on <m>f^{-1}</m>!)
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </assemblage>

    
    <p>
        Graphically, an inverse function is a reflection across the line
        <m>{y=x}</m>.
        See the picture of <m>f</m> (the top curve) and <m>f^{-1}</m> (the
        bottom curve) below, and the line <m>{y=x}</m> between them.
        (Graph is not for the next activity.)
    </p>
    <image width="30%">
      <shortdescription>A graph f(x) and it's inverse shown on one graph, along with the reflecting line y=x.</shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-1-7-4">
 <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="10">
  <definition>f(x)= 2^x</definition>
  <definition>g(x)= ln(x)/ln(2) </definition>
  <definition>h(x)=x</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-5,-5,5,5]">
    <grid-axes />
    <graph function="f"/>
    <graph function="g" />
    <graph function="h" dash="4 4"/>
    <point p="(-1,1/2)" fill="blue" alignment="nw" ><m>(-1,\frac12)</m></point>
    <point p="(1/2,-1)" fill="blue" alignment="se" ><m>(\frac12,-1)</m></point>



    <label anchor="(-2,4.5)"><m>y = f(x)</m></label>
    <label anchor="(2,-4.5)"><m>y = f^{-1}(x)</m></label>

  </coordinates>
</diagram>
                        </prefigure>

    </image>


    
   

    <activity>
        <title>Activity 1.7.4</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                During a major rainstorm, the rainfall at Gerald R.
                Ford Airport is measured on a frequent basis for a 10-hour
                period of time.
                The following function <m>g</m> models the <term>rate</term>,
                <m>R</m>, at which the rain falls (in cm/hr) on the time
                interval <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=10</m>:
                <md>
                    R = g(t) = \frac{4}{t+2} +1.
                </md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute <m>g(3)</m> and write a complete sentence to explain
                    its meaning in the given context, including units.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                   Plug in: <m>g(3) = \frac4{3+2}+1 = \frac45+1 = \frac9.5</m> (or 1.8)
                   cm/hr.  Meaning: At time 3 or 3 hours into measuring, the 
                   rain is falling at a rate of <m>\frac95</m> cm per hour.  (We expect
                   about <m>1.8</m> cm of rain for the whole hour starting at hour 3.)
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Compute the average rate of change of <m>g</m> on the time
                    interval <m>[3,5]</m> and write two careful complete
                    sentences to explain the meaning of this value in the
                    context of the problem, including units.
                    Explicitly address what the value you compute tells you
                    about how rain is falling over a certain time interval, and
                    what you should expect as time goes on.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    Average rate of change formula (i.e. slope between the point when 
                    <m>x=3</m> and <m>x=5</m>!): 
                    <md>AV_{[3,5]} = \frac{g(5)-g(3)}{5-3} = -0.115 \frac{cm/hr}{hr}.</md>
                    On average between hours 3 and 5 the rate of rain fall is decreasing by 
                    -0.115 cm / hr / hr.  Or in other words, the rate is slowing down. 
                    The rain falls faster at the beginning of the interval than at the
                    end of the interval.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Plot the function <m>y=g(t)</m> using a computational device
                    (Desmos?).
                    On the domain <m>[0,10]</m>, what is the corresponding range
                    of <m>g</m>?
                    Why does the function <m>g</m> have an inverse function?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    Restrict to <m>0\le t \le 10</m> and ignore the other times. 
                    The range is then <m>[\frac43, 3]</m>.  It passes the horizontal line
                    test.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Determine <m>g^{-1}(\frac95)</m> and write a complete
                    sentence to explain its meaning in the given context.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    So <m>g^{-1}(\frac95)=3 hrs</m>. When the rate of 
                    rain fall is <m>\frac95</m> cm/hr, the time is 3 hours.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task workspace="1in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    According to the model <m>g</m>, is there ever a time during
                    the storm that the rain falls at a rate of exactly <m>1</m>
                    centimeter per hour?
                    Why or why not?
                    Provide an algebraic justification for your answer.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <solution component="S1-7-soln">
                <p>
                    Nope!  You can see it from the graph or if <m>R=1</m>, 
                    then <m>1=\frac{4}{t+2}+1</m>, solving for <m>t</m> gives 
                    <m>0=\frac{4}{t+1}</m>, or in other words
                    <m>0=4</m> is not possible.
                </p>
            </solution>
        </task>
    </activity>
</handout>